WEBVTT

00:00:03.300 --> 00:00:07.670
So, Claude Code just fixed one bug, and then it stopped.

00:00:07.700 --> 00:00:09.070
There's still one left.

00:00:10.020 --> 00:00:12.180
Now normally, you'd open another agent,

00:00:12.180 --> 00:00:14.670
and it starts from zero.

00:00:15.900 --> 00:00:18.060
So you explain everything…

00:00:18.060 --> 00:00:19.310
all over again.

00:00:20.140 --> 00:00:21.340
But with Continuum?

00:00:21.340 --> 00:00:22.540
It doesn't have to.

00:00:23.800 --> 00:00:26.120
You just connect each agent once.

00:00:26.120 --> 00:00:28.050
One command each, that's it.

00:00:29.280 --> 00:00:32.640
And while Claude's working, Continuum keeps track of the task,

00:00:32.640 --> 00:00:35.450
what's done, and, you know, what comes next.

00:00:36.630 --> 00:00:39.280
So when Codex starts up, it's already briefed.

00:00:39.350 --> 00:00:43.200
What's done, what's left, and exactly what to do next.

00:00:44.200 --> 00:00:47.490
Codex just picks up the same task, and fixes the second bug.

00:00:48.560 --> 00:00:50.360
Then you run the tests through Continuum,

00:00:50.360 --> 00:00:52.560
and the result is actually verified,

00:00:52.560 --> 00:00:54.490
against the exact code it ran on.

00:00:56.220 --> 00:00:57.510
And over in the dashboard?

00:00:57.580 --> 00:01:00.500
It's all one task, carried from one agent…

00:01:00.500 --> 00:01:01.700
to the next.

00:01:03.120 --> 00:01:04.490
Your work continues.

00:01:04.560 --> 00:01:05.930
Your agents change.

00:01:06.930 --> 00:01:10.860
That's Continuum. It's free, and it runs right on your machine.
